<!-- /wp:image --></div>
<!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {"className":"hk-row__text sp-reveal sp-reveal--right","layout":{"type":"default"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group hk-row__text sp-reveal sp-reveal--right"><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"hk-name"} -->
<p class="hk-name">Camille Laurent</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"hk-role"} -->
<p class="hk-role">Food-Truck Owner · Cohort 12</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph {"className":"hk-body"} -->
<p class="hk-body">Camille Laurent owns and operates an independent food truck serving grilled chicken wraps, seasoned grain bowls, and cold side salads across event locations. In a compact mobile kitchen with limited refrigeration and prep space, maintaining strict temperature separation was her primary challenge. After completing Cohort 12, Camille restructured her prep line around five daily probe checks. She calibrated her probe in ice water every Monday morning, verified raw meat delivery temperatures upon arrival, and logged rapid cooling cycles for pre-cooked chicken batches. The practical routines gave Camille confidence that her food safety records were airtight, even during chaotic weekend music festivals.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
